[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Modern Typography: Fluid Typography, Clamp, Viewport Units, and Variable Fonts in CSS3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditional web design relied on rigid media queries to change font sizes at specific breakpoints. Today, CSS3 allows us to create seamlessly scaling typography that adapts fluidly to any screen size using modern viewport units, the clamp() function, and variable fonts."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Modern Viewport Units"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Standard viewport units (vw, vh) have historically caused layout issues on mobile devices when the browser's address bar hides or shows. CSS3 introduces new viewport units to solve this problem:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"svw / svh: Small viewport size (accounts for expanded UI elements like address bars)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"lvw / lvh: Large viewport size (assumes UI elements are hidden)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"dvw / dvh: Dynamic viewport size (adjusts dynamically as UI elements appear and disappear)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Magic of CSS clamp()"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The clamp() function takes three parameters: a minimum value, a preferred value (usually a viewport unit), and a maximum value. This prevents your typography from becoming too small on mobile phones or excessively large on ultra-wide monitors."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"h1 {
  font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw + 1rem, 3.5rem);
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the font size will never drop below 1.5rem, will scale smoothly based on 3vw plus 1rem, and will never exceed 3.5rem."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Integrating Variable Fonts"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Variable fonts allow multiple variations of a typeface (weight, width, slant) to be incorporated into a single file. We can animate or transition these axes using standard CSS properties like font-variation-settings."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"@font-face {
  font-family: 'InterVariable';
  src: url('InterVariable.woff2') format('woff2');
  font-weight: 100 900;
}

.fluid-heading {
  font-family: 'InterVariable', sans-serif;
  font-variation-settings: 'wght' 400, 'opsz' 32;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Putting It All Together"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining clamp(), dynamic viewport units, and variable fonts, you can create a truly resilient typographic hierarchy that responds organically to the user's viewport without writing a single media query."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":":root {
  --fluid-min: 1rem;
  --fluid-preferred: 2.5vw;
  --fluid-max: 2.5rem;
}

body {
  font-size: clamp(var(--fluid-min), var(--fluid-preferred), var(--fluid-max));
  font-variation-settings: 'wght' calc(300 + (5vw));
}"}]
